Frontend: Live Subtitles for Autonomous Play + "AI Game Streamer" UI
Context
The autonomous Slay the Spire 2 play MVP (previous phase, complete and verified — Go core + services/cognitive + sts2_poller.py) now works end-to-end: /game_start hands control to the LLM, which calls sts2_* tools to play the game and narrates each move. The user confirmed this live (screenshot of the AIRI frontend running beside a real STS2 window).

Testing surfaced a real frontend bug: during autonomous play, the backend's spoken commentary (agent.text_delta over the WebSocket) never appears as chat bubbles or subtitles. The user self-diagnosed the likely cause (no active "Turn" since nothing called ingest()) and asked for two things:

Required: make agent.text_delta render live even when no user-initiated turn is active.
Strongly recommended: turn this into a proper "AI game streamer" surface — a floating subtitle overlay near the character, a live Floor/HP/Gold mini-dashboard, and gaze/head-tilt bias toward the game instead of following the mouse.
Investigation (this phase) confirmed the diagnosis but found it's worse than one gate — there are two independent, stacked blockers, one of which is a standing dead-code bug that has silently never worked.

Part 1 (required): render agent.text_delta without an active turn
Root cause, confirmed by direct read of the code (not just the prior research pass):

frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/stores/chat.ts:461-475 — a global, always-on bridge.onTextDelta listener already exists, registered once via a setInterval-until-bridge-exists poll. But its body calls runtime.onTokenLiteral?.({ text, metadata: {} }) — runtime (the object returned by createChatOrchestratorRuntime) has no onTokenLiteral property. Only runtime.hooks.onTokenLiteral exists (confirmed: it's what line 537 exposes as the store's own onTokenLiteral). The optional-chaining means this has always been a silent no-op — this listener has never done anything, for any turn, ever.
Even if fixed, frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/components/scenarios/chat/components/history.vue:60-62 — renderMessages's very first line is if (!props.sending) return props.messages, unconditionally dropping the streaming message whenever sending is false. sending only ever becomes true via performSend() (chat-orchestrator-runtime.ts), which is never invoked for backend-pushed autonomous-play text.
A separate, already-working per-turn listener exists inside streamWithStageAdapters (chat.ts:152-182) — registered only while a user-initiated turn (via ingest()/performSend()) is awaiting its WS round trip, torn down on completion. This is what makes normal chat "just work" today. During a normal turn, both listeners are live simultaneously — the fix below must not double-append text when that happens.
Fix:

chat.ts: replace the dead runtime.onTokenLiteral?.() call in the global listener (lines 461-475) with logic that:
does nothing while sending.value is true (the per-turn listener already owns rendering for that case — this is the anti-double-append guard);
otherwise treats this as an unsolicited/autonomous stream: on the first delta of a burst, call useChatStreamStore().beginStream(); on every delta, appendStreamLiteral(text); when isFinal arrives, finalizeStream() (which appends the completed message into chatSession.appendSessionMessage at the current activeSessionId — the exact same store method performSend() itself relies on) and reset the "stream open" flag. Track "stream open" with one local let in the store closure — no new store state needed.
add a safety-net timer (mirroring the existing 15s pattern at chat.ts:177-181) that force-finalizes if is_final never arrives, so a backend hiccup can't wedge the stream open forever.
history.vue: relax renderMessages so a non-empty streamingMessage also renders when sending is false, while leaving sending === true behavior (including the "thinking" placeholder path) completely untouched:
const renderMessages = computed<ChatHistoryItem[]>(() => {
  const streamTs = streamingTs.value
  if (!streamTs)
    return props.messages

  const hasStreamContent = (streaming.value.slices?.length ?? 0) > 0 || !!streaming.value.content
  if (!props.sending && !hasStreamContent)
    return props.messages

  const hasStreamAlready = props.messages.some(msg => msg?.role === 'assistant' && msg?.createdAt === streamTs)
  if (hasStreamAlready)
    return props.messages

  return [...props.messages, streaming.value]
})
This is safe because finalizeStream() resets streamingMessage to an object with no createdAt, so streamingTs.value becomes undefined and the early return above still fires — no phantom bubble lingers after a stream ends, regardless of sending.
Files: frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/stores/chat.ts, frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/components/scenarios/chat/components/history.vue.

Noted but explicitly out of scope for this pass: bridge.onEmotion's global handler (chat.ts:469-471) has the exact same dead-code shape (runtime.onTokenSpecial?.()), so autonomous-play emotion signals currently drive no Live2D expression either. Same bug class, but the user's ask was specifically about text/subtitles — flagging it rather than bundling it in, since the correct consumer for a fixed emotion signal needs its own look before touching it.

Part 2 (recommended): AI game streamer UI
2a. Floating subtitle overlay
New frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/components/gadgets/live-caption-overlay.vue, a thin wrapper around the existing (currently unused outside its own story) chat-bubble-minimalism.vue (frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/components/gadgets/chat-bubble-minimalism.vue — already takes text/side/loading props and does its own reveal animation via PoppinText).
Reads text directly from useChatStreamStore().streamingMessage — the same store Part 1 now correctly drives for both normal chat and autonomous commentary, so this is a pure new "view," no new data plumbing.
Mount point: frontend/apps/stage-web/src/pages/index.vue, inside the existing <div relative flex-1 min-w="1/2"> character container (~line 220), absolutely positioned near the bottom of the Live2D canvas, large/punchy text per the user's "AI主播" framing.
2b. STS2 mini-dashboard (Floor / HP / Gold)
No channel currently pushes live game stats to the browser — this needs one small new backend leg, following the exact pattern already established for /api/game-turn (loopback-only listener, GAME_EVENT_TOKEN auth via X-Game-Event-Token, target-chat resolved through AutonomousPlayState.TargetChatID()) and for pushing to the browser (b.sessions.SendTextToChat, the same primitive agent.text_delta/agent.emotion already use — confirmed in core/internal/webgateway/nats_bridge.go).

services/game_watcher/sts2_poller.py: after every successful poll (not just ACTIONABLE_STATE_TYPES), compute a light snapshot (floor, hp, max_hp, gold, act) and POST it when it changes — new RunTracker.last_state_signature field, debounced the same way last_turn_signature already is. New post_game_state(...) helper, structurally identical to the existing post_game_turn(...) (sts2_poller.py:151-181).
Go: new core/internal/webgateway/game_state_handler.go, POST /api/game-state on the same dedicated loopback listener/token as handleGameTurn. Resolves target chat via s.autonomousPlayState.TargetChatID() exactly like game_turn_handler.go:67-70 (no CSM busy-check needed — this isn't triggering a reasoning turn). On success, marshals a new agent.game_state WSMessage ({floor, hp, max_hp, gold, act}) and calls s.bridge.sessions.SendTextToChat(targetChatID, outBytes). Register the route in server.go next to /api/game-turn.
Frontend: betteragent-ws.ts gets a GameStateCallback type, a gameStateListeners set, a case 'agent.game_state': branch in handleMessage, and an onGameState() subscribe method — exact mirror of the existing onEmotion/onTextDelta pair (betteragent-ws.ts:258-266).
New small Pinia store frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/stores/modules/sts2-game-state.ts: subscribes to betterAgentWSBridge.onGameState(...) the same way chat.ts waits for the bridge singleton; holds { floor, hp, maxHp, gold, act, lastUpdatedAt }; exposes isLikelyActive (Date.now() - lastUpdatedAt < ~15s).
New frontend/packages/stage-ui/src/components/gadgets/sts2-dashboard.vue: HP rendered via the existing level-meter.vue gadget (:level="hp" :max="maxHp" — its bar/threshold-color visual is a direct fit), Floor/Gold/Act as small adjacent badges. Rendered only when isLikelyActive.
Mount point: same index.vue character container, opposite corner from the caption overlay (e.g. top-right) so they don't collide.
2c. Gaze bias toward the game window
index.vue's existing cursorPosition computed (lines 198-202) is the one hook that already feeds WidgetStage → Live2D.vue's focusAt prop → pixi-live2d-display's native model.focus(x, y). No Live2D-internals changes needed.
When sts2GameState.isLikelyActive is true, override with a fixed bias point (e.g. { x: window.innerWidth * 0.9, y: window.innerHeight * 0.5 } — simulating a game window sitting to her right) instead of the live mouse position; otherwise unchanged mouse-follow.
